            'Tidal resonance is the dominant mechanism behind extreme tidal ranges in estuaries like the Bay of Fundy (tidal range up to 16 m (52 ft), the world\'s highest) and the Severn (up to ~15 m (49 ft)). A tidal wave entering a semi-enclosed basin of length L will reflect off the closed end and travel back toward the open ocean. If the travel time of the tidal wave from mouth to head and back again is exactly equal to the tidal period (roughly 12.4 hours for M2), the reflected wave is precisely in phase with the next incoming tidal wave — the system is at resonance. At resonance, the tidal amplitude grows with each cycle until it is limited by frictional dissipation. The condition for a quarter-wavelength resonator (the simplest case, equivalent to an organ pipe open at one end) is L = λ/4, where λ = c × T (c is the shallow-water wave speed, T is the tidal period). For the Bay of Fundy, L ≈ 270 km (168 mi) and the resonant period is very close to the dominant M2 tidal period, producing near-maximum amplification. Option D describes a real and important contributing process — the funnel geometry of estuaries does concentrate the tidal prism and can amplify tidal range — and is sometimes cited alongside resonance. However, the funnel geometry alone cannot explain the extraordinary tidal ranges in these specific locations; many funnel-shaped estuaries have modest tidal ranges. The key is the resonance match, which is why the Bay of Fundy has the world\'s largest tides while similarly shaped inlets do not. Option A describes a real fluvial-tidal interaction but incorrectly identifies it as the primary amplification mechanism; river flow can modify tidal propagation but is not the cause of 15–16 m (49–52 ft) tidal ranges. Option C is physically incorrect: the tidal bulge is a global feature of ocean response to gravitational forcing; continental shelves do influence tidal propagation (shelf resonance is related to the resonance mechanism), but the claim that some locations are "directly beneath the tidal bulge" more than others on the time-averaged basis needed to explain persistent large tidal ranges is incorrect.',
