          correct: 1,
          explain:
            'The Arctic and Antarctic sea ice systems are fundamentally different, and those differences cascade through ecosystems and climate. The Arctic Ocean is a semi-enclosed basin roughly the size of Russia, surrounded by the North American, Eurasian, and Greenland landmasses. This confinement allows sea ice to persist through summer in the Central Arctic, building up multi-year sea ice (MYI) that can reach 3–5 m (10–16 ft) thickness. MYI supports a more stable under-ice ecosystem and serves as resting and hunting habitat for polar bears. The Arctic has been losing MYI rapidly under climate change. In contrast, the Antarctic continent sits over the South Pole, and Antarctic sea ice forms entirely over the open Southern Ocean — there are no confining land boundaries to retain it. As a result, Antarctic sea ice is almost entirely first-year ice: it grows from roughly 3 million km² in late summer to a maximum of ~18 million km² by winter and then retreats again, an annual cycle of growth and melt that involves roughly 15 million km² of ice on average. This dynamic creates ecologically important seasonal transitions: the underside of winter sea ice provides an algae-rich habitat for krill and other organisms that would otherwise starve through the polar night; the spring melt creates strong halocline stratification from ice meltwater, forming a shallow, stable mixed layer that fuels intense phytoplankton blooms (the marginal ice zone bloom) that are among the Southern Ocean\'s most productive events. Option A contains multiple errors: both Arctic and Antarctic sea ice are compositionally similar (both form brine-rich ice with brine channels that drain as the ice ages); neither forms from "freshwater runoff" — sea ice forms by freezing seawater. Option C gives an incorrect freezing point for Antarctic seawater; the freezing point of seawater is approximately −1.8°C (35°F) at 35 psu regardless of hemisphere — salinity sets the freezing point depression, and both hemispheres have similar surface salinities. Option D is empirically incorrect: the Arctic and Antarctic sea ice systems differ substantially in extent, thickness, seasonal variability, ice type, surrounding geography, and ecological communities; they are not symmetric or ecologically equivalent.',
